[FINAL-TEXT] ## Phase 3 — OSPF Snapshot Analysis

The `ospf_snapshot` output is definitive across all three routers:

| Router | ospfd running? | ospfd VTY socket? | OSPF networks | Neighbors | OSPF routes |
|--------|---------------|-------------------|--------------|-----------|-------------|
| router0 | **NO** | **NO** | none | 0 | 0 |
| router1 | **NO** | **NO** | none | 0 | 0 |
| router2 | **NO** | **NO** | none | 0 | 0 |

**Key facts:**
- `ospfd=no` + `ospfd_vty=no` on all three routers — the OSPF daemon is not running anywhere (note: `watchfrr=no` / `zebra=no` are confirmed baseline noise in Kathara per the snapshot note; `zebra_vty=yes` confirms zebra/routing manager itself is alive)
- No OSPF `network` statements configured on any router
- Zero neighbors, zero OSPF-learned routes on all routers

This perfectly explains the observed failure pattern: each router can only reach its **directly connected subnets**. Router2 (which serves `lan_servers` 1.0.0.x and `lan_internet` 128.168.0.x) has no route to 192.168.x.x — hence the `Destination Net Unreachable` ICMP from 1.0.0.1/128.168.0.1. Router1 and router0 have no routes to 128.168.0.x/1.0.0.x — hence the timeouts. Within-zone traffic (e.g. `office_pc → pc0`, `pc1 → pc3`) works because those use router-local directly-connected subnets.

The confirming evidence is sufficient. Advancing to Phase 5.

